  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1127 PM EST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nA low pressure system developing off the southeast coast will \nstrengthen and track north and inland across the eastern US today \nand tonight. Meanwhile an upper low to our west will track across \nour area today and then off to our northeast. This system will \nbring gusty winds at times, as well as rain for our entire area \nthat will transition to some light snow at least over the higher \nelevations late today into Friday. \n\nThe ongoing windy conditions over the higher terrain of the eastern \nmountains will begin to subside from southwest to northeast over the \nnext several hours and should subside over even our far northeastern \nmountains this morning.  While observed winds have been \nunderperforming a bit based on the forecast, the current wind \nadvisory for these mountain areas will be allowed to continue as is \nwith just a tweak down in wind speeds.  After a lull in the gusty \nwinds, we will see winds increase again later today as the pressure \ngradient tightens around the back side of the low pressure system to \nour northeast. This round of strong gusty winds will continue into \nFriday morning and will again be strongest in the higher elevations \nof the eastern mountains, but we will see gusty winds elsewhere \nacross our area especially portions of the central valley, northern \nvalley and SW VA where gusts of 30 to 40 mph are expected. A second \nwind advisory for late this afternoon through Friday morning has \nbeen issued for the mountains and SW VA as well as portions of the \ncentral and northern valley. As mentioned when it was issued, \nalthough this is a bit on the low side for wind advisory criteria, \nit was decided to issue this second wind advisory due to the higher \nlikelihood of impacts due to Thanksgiving travel and family \ngatherings. Areas not in the wind advisory are still expected to see \ngusts from 20 to 30+ mph at times. \n\nAs for the precipitation, the rain will continue tonight into \ntomorrow, and latest QPF values generally fall in the half inch to \n1.25 inch range across our area. As colder air pushes in on the back \nside of the low pressure system and the upper low tracks overhead, \nwe will see freezing levels fall with rain changing to snow across \nthe higher elevations of the Plateau and mountains late Thursday \ninto Thursday evening, and eventually even valley locations may see \nsome wet snowflakes Thursday night into Friday morning. Valley \nlocations look unlikely to see any accumulation given the warm \nground and also surface temperatures that will bottom out around or \njust above freezing, but higher elevations will likely see light \nsnow accumulations. Low level flow will generally be from the \nsouthwest then west during this time so it does not look favorable \nfor much of an orographic boost, so right now it continues to look \nlike minor accumulations around 1 to 2 inches are possible across \nthe higher elevations of the east TN, southwest VA, and southwest NC \nmountains through Friday. A few of the highest peaks may see more. \nThis agrees pretty well with the latest runs of the REFS. As of now, \nno winter weather advisory will be issued due to the low probability \nof even minor impacts, but this will bear watching in case there is \na trend toward more impactful snow. \n\nAny lingering light precipitation over our northeastern higher \nterrain will end early Friday night, then Saturday will be dry with \nseasonable temperatures.  Another system will likely bring rain \nSunday into Sunday night. Wind fields look likely to be significant \nagain with this system so strong gusty winds will be possible \nespecially across the higher elevations. Ensemble data suggests \nconvective energy is likely to be minimal, but there may be enough \ninstability for a few rumbles of thunder Sunday. \n\nAfter we clear out on Monday, the remainder of the forecast through \nWednesday looks dry with near seasonal temperatures.",
